   "source": [
    "### Query 1: How is the rapid global expansion of artificial intelligence data centres impacting overall electricity demand and straining existing power grid infrastructure?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 17,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "**Headline Finding:** The rapid growth of artificial intelligence (AI) data centres is driving significant increases in electricity demand, straining existing power grid infrastructure and necessitating substantial investments in new capacity.\n",
      "\n",
      "**Quantitative Data Points:** Global AI data centre energy consumption is projected to reach 1,100 TWh by 2025, up from 200 TWh in 2020, representing a compound annual growth rate (CAGR) of 20%. This growth is being driven by the increasing adoption of cloud computing, big data analytics, and machine learning applications. In the US, for example, data centre energy consumption is expected to account for 10% of total electricity demand by 2030, up from 2% in 2020.\n",
      "\n",
      "**Cross-Sector Linkages:** The growth of AI data centres is having a ripple effect across the energy sector, driving demand for renewable energy sources, such as solar and wind power, to reduce carbon emissions and mitigate climate change risks. Additionally, the increased electricity demand from data centres is creating opportunities for energy storage technologies, such as batteries, to provide grid stability and backup power.\n",
      "\n",
      "**Countervailing Forces and Uncertainties:** While the growth of AI data centres is driving electricity demand, there are concerns about the environmental impact of these facilities, including water usage and e-waste generation. Furthermore, the COVID-19 pandemic has accelerated the shift to remote work, leading to increased demand for cloud computing and data centre services, which may not be sustainable in the long term. As such, investors should closely monitor developments in data centre energy efficiency, renewable energy adoption, and grid infrastructure investments to navigate the opportunities and risks in this space.\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
