      "\n",
      "**Cross-Sector Linkages:**\n",
      "The shift towards domestic feedstocks for SAF production will increase demand for renewable biomass and waste feedstocks, potentially competing with traditional biomass markets. This, in turn, may drive up feedstock costs for other biofuel producers. Additionally, the growth of SAF production will lead to increased demand for hydrogen, which may be sourced from natural gas, electricity, or other low-carbon sources, affecting these markets.\n",
      "\n",
      "**Countervailing Forces and Uncertainties:**\n",
      "- Feedstock availability and cost volatility may impact the economic viability of domestic SAF production.\n",
      "- The pace of technology advancements in SAF production and competing low-carbon aviation fuels may influence the transition timeline.\n",
      "- Regulatory developments, such as changes to the Renewable Fuel Standard (RFS) or carbon pricing mechanisms, may further impact the investment economics of SAF production.\n",
      "\n",
      "**Cascading Impact on US West Coast Refineries:**\n",
      "The increased focus on domestic feedstocks for SAF production may lead to a 10-15% reduction in traditional refining capacity on the US West Coast by 2027, as refineries repurpose or retire existing infrastructure to accommodate growing SAF demand.\n"
     ]
