  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "rJSNDez0ZiE7"
   },
   "source": [
    "### Query 1: How is the rapid global expansion of artificial intelligence data centres impacting overall electricity demand and straining existing power grid infrastructure?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 34,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "**Headline Finding**: The rapid global expansion of artificial intelligence (AI) data centres is driving electricity demand growth, straining existing power grid infrastructure, and prompting governments to introduce regulatory frameworks to accommodate rising grid connection requests.\n",
      "\n",
      "**Supporting Data**: Electricity demand is expected to outpace economic growth globally through 2030, with AI data centres being a key driver [Electricity2026 p.12]. In the United States, only around 20% of data centre connection requests materialise in the short to medium term [Electricity2026 p.59]. The UK government has introduced the AI Growth Zones regulatory package to prioritise grid access for strategically important AI data centres [Electricity2026 p.61].\n",
      "\n",
      "**Cross-Sector Linkages**: The growth of AI data centres is linked to the increasing demand for cloud services and technological innovations, which are driving electricity use in advanced economies [Electricity2026 p.12]. Governments are responding by introducing frameworks to manage grid connection requests and incentivise grid optimisation, such as the UK's AI Growth Zones package [Electricity2026 p.61] and Canada's Load Reliability-based grid access limit [Electricity2026 p.58].\n",
      "\n",
      "**Countervailing Forces**: While AI data centres are driving electricity demand growth, technologies such as advanced power flow control devices and flexible AC transmission systems can increase grid capacity and reduce congestion [Electricity2026 p.65]. However, realising these capacity gains requires integrating these technologies into grid planning and operational processes, supported by appropriate regulatory frameworks [Electricity2026 p.64].\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Strategy: tuned_rag  \u2014  benchmark query 1\n",
    "answer = CACHED_ANSWERS['tuned_rag']['answers'][0]\n",
    "print(answer)\n"
   ]
