   "source": [
    "### Query 3: How are the surge in US electricity demand and the 2025 federal emergency policy interventions collectively affecting the retirement schedules, capacity planning, and generation output of domestic coal-fired power plants?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 36,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "**Headline Finding**: The surge in US electricity demand and 2025 federal emergency policy interventions are collectively affecting the retirement schedules, capacity planning, and generation output of domestic coal-fired power plants by slowing down planned retirements and potentially sustaining coal availability longer than previously assumed.\n",
      "\n",
      "**Supporting Data**: \n",
      "- Planned retirements have slowed markedly, with latest reports of capacity to be retired in 2025 totaling 6.2 GW, well below earlier projections of 15 GW [Coal2025 p.30].\n",
      "- The policy environment has shifted in favor of coal in the near term, with executive actions reaffirming coal's role in national energy security and directing agencies to accelerate permitting and reduce regulatory barriers [Coal2025 p.29].\n",
      "- Federal policies, including executive orders and the DOI's leasing initiatives, could delay retirements and sustain coal availability longer than previously assumed [Coal2025 p.31].\n",
      "- Coal production in the United States is projected to be 473 Mt in 2025, driven by higher coal demand and policy actions [Coal2025 p.54].\n",
      "\n",
      "**Cross-Sector Linkages**: The surge in US electricity demand is driven by the continuing emergence of AI and its growing demand for electricity [Coal2025 p.55]. The policy interventions are also affecting the coal supply chain, with the DOI expanding leasing opportunities on federal lands and the DOE releasing additional acreage for new leasing [Coal2025 p.29].\n",
      "\n",
      "**Countervailing Forces**: The transformation of blast furnaces into production with a lower CO2 footprint may face economic hurdles, which could limit the impact of policy interventions on coal demand [Coal2025 p.31]. Additionally, global coal production is forecast to decline slightly through 2030, which could affect international markets and potentially counterbalance the effects of US policy interventions [Coal2025 p.8].\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
